==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{sense|attraction to soldiers}} In allusion to the red coats they used to wear.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|disease}} A [[streptococcal]] infection, mainly occurring among children, and characterized by a red skin rash, sore throat and fever.
#* {{RQ:Landon Lady Anne Granard|page=54|volume=I|passage="I am sure," replied Mrs. Palmer, "if it were not wicked, I should be glad that you had the '''scarlet fever'''; I am so much happier since I knew you...}}
#: {{synonyms|en|scarlatina}}
# {{lb|en|UK|slang|obsolete|uncountable}} Romantic attraction to [[soldier]]s in preference to other people.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2019|author=Hallie Rubenhold|title=The Five|page=79
|passage=According to Henry Mayhew, it was here that housemaids and nursemaids walking in the park to and from their place of work often first encountered “the all powerful red coat” and “succumbed to '''Scarlet Fever'''.” Soldiers were by no means unaware of the impact their dashing uniforms and well-groomed military air could have on young women, and deployed these features to their advantage.}}

